# Political Comms integration rules

You are integrating with Political Comms, the direct-to-carrier political texting platform.

## API basics
- Base URL: https://api.politicalcomms.com/v1
- Auth: X-API-Key header. Keys are prefixed pc_live_ and are generated by a human in the dashboard (app.politicalcomms.com, Admin then API Keys). Keys are shown once.
- Official SDKs: @political-comms/sdk (npm, TypeScript), political-comms (PyPI), @political-comms/cli, and @political-comms/mcp (MCP server). Direct HTTP against the spec works equally well.
- Source of truth for endpoints: https://docs.politicalcomms.com/api-reference/openapi.json
- Docs: https://docs.politicalcomms.com/api-reference/introduction
- Full machine-readable reference: https://politicalcomms.com/llms-full.txt

## Rules
- Always consult the OpenAPI spec before writing a request. Never fabricate endpoints, parameters, or response fields.
- Read API keys from environment variables or a secret manager. Never hardcode keys, never commit them, never print them in logs or output.
- Respect the rate limits: per key over a 60-second sliding window: 100 requests/minute for reads, 60/minute for writes, 30/minute for deletes. Read the X-RateLimit-* response headers and back off before the ceiling.
- Errors are structured JSON: {success, error, code, statusCode}. Branch on code and statusCode, not on the error string. Do not retry 4xx with the same payload. Retry 5xx with exponential backoff.
- Set an Idempotency-Key header (a UUID) on write requests you might retry, especially POST /projects/{id}/schedule.
- Sending is two calls: POST /projects (organization_id, name, protocol, message_text, brand_id + campaign_id on the 10dlc channel, phone_number_ids, contact_list_ids), then POST /projects/{id}/schedule (scheduled_at with UTC offset + scheduled_timezone). Validation is strict: unknown body properties are rejected with a 400.
- Webhooks: validate the HMAC-SHA256 signature in X-Webhook-Signature (format sha256=...) against the raw request body using a constant-time comparison before trusting any payload. Return 2xx quickly; non-2xx triggers retries, so make handlers idempotent.
- Webhook events: message.sent, message.delivered, message.failed, message.replied, link.clicked. Email adds email.delivered, email.bounced, email.complained, email.unsubscribed, email.clicked (early access).
- Email (/v1/email: domains, senders, lists, list imports, suppressions, campaigns, templates) is EARLY ACCESS: every endpoint returns 403 EMAIL_EARLY_ACCESS until general availability. That is expected, not a bad key. Do not retry it. Email lists are keyset paginated ({data, has_more, next_cursor}), unlike the messaging endpoints; page until next_cursor is null and never parse a cursor. There is no inbox, no inbound email, no A/B testing, and no email.opened event. DNS is manual: publish the records the API returns. Removing list contacts unsubscribes them rather than deleting them. Read the blocked array from GET /v1/email/campaigns/{id} before scheduling, and never retry a resume that returns 409 EMAIL_CAMPAIGN_RESUME_REQUIRES_SUPPORT: escalate to a human.
- Lincoln drafts email templates asynchronously and for money: POST /v1/email/templates/drafts returns 202 with a draft id and unit_price ($3.00 default), then poll GET /v1/email/templates/drafts/{id} until status is ready or failed (usually under two minutes, poll no faster than every 2 seconds). Only a ready draft is billed; a failed one never is, so request a new draft rather than retrying. 402 INSUFFICIENT_BALANCE up front means no draft was created. Draft images must be organization-owned media with usage email_asset (POST /v1/media accepts usage mms|email_asset; brand_id is not allowed with email_asset). POST /v1/email/lists/import imports a CSV you host over HTTPS in one call and returns 202; poll GET /v1/email/lists/imports/{id}, and when it returns 400 VALIDATION_ERROR read details.headers and send a mapping naming the email column. Template writes return a lint object: errors there do not block the save but do block scheduling a campaign that uses the template.
- For documentation lookups, use the Political Comms MCP server (https://docs.politicalcomms.com/mcp, manifest at https://politicalcomms.com/.well-known/mcp.json) or fetch https://politicalcomms.com/llms.txt.
